  "response": "## What the finding says\n\n`crime_00029` — **SVG With Embedded Raster**, `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`, severity high, confidence 0.95. From its evidence:\n\n- **\"2 `<image href=\"data:image/…;base64,…\">` occurrences\"** — two embedded rasters\n- **\"embedded MIME types: image/jpeg, image/png\"**\n- \"SVG byte size: 592 bytes (includes the base64 overhead)\"\n\nThe file itself confirms the layout — a 200×80 canvas with two 80×80 marks and a 20px gutter:\n\n```\n<svg width=\"200\" height=\"80\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 80\">\n  <title>Partner logo</title>\n  <image x=\"0\"   y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" href=\"data:image/png;base64,…\"/>\n  <image x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" xlink:href=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,…\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n## Which half goes which way\n\nThe encoding choice is the tell, and it splits the two regions cleanly:\n\n**The PNG at `x=0` → re-author as vector paths.** PNG is the lossless/flat-colour format; a designer reaches for it when the artwork is a logo mark — solid fills, hard edges, a bounded palette. That is exactly what `<path>` geometry represents natively. Pull the original from Figma/Illustrator/Sketch and export *that* layer as SVG rather than tracing the 80×80 bitmap, so you get the real curves instead of an autotrace of resampled pixels.\n\n**The JPEG at `x=100` → split out to a standalone raster asset.** JPEG is lossy DCT compression, chosen for continuous-tone content: a photo, a gradient, a soft-shaded emblem. There is no faithful vector equivalent — tracing it produces either a blurry mesh or a thousand-path file bigger than the photo. It stays a raster; it just stops living inside the SVG.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Get the vector source for the PNG mark.** Export the layer as SVG from the design tool, or vectorise it if the source is genuinely lost. Verify at 4× zoom that edges stay crisp.\n2. **Inline that geometry into `partner-logo.svg`**, replacing the first `<image>` — `<path d=\"…\"/>` (grouped under a `<g transform=\"translate(0,0)\">` if the export has its own coordinate space) so it lands in the same 0–80 slot of the `viewBox`.\n3. **Decode the JPEG payload to a real file.** `base64 -d` the data URI into `src/assets/partner-photo.jpg`, then re-encode to WebP or AVIF at the dimensions it actually renders (80×80 at 1×, so export 160×160 for 2× DPI).\n4. **Decide how the photo is composed back in** — this is the one decision that isn't mechanical, see below.\n5. **Delete both `data:` URIs.** Also drop the `xlink:href` on the second image; `xlink` is deprecated in SVG 2, plain `href` is correct.\n6. **Re-run `crimes scan --format json`** and confirm `svg_with_embedded_raster` on this path is gone.\n\n## The composition decision (step 4)\n\nThe tempting move is to leave the `<image>` element and point it at the new file:\n\n```\n<image x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" href=\"./partner-photo.webp\"/>\n```\n\n**That only works if the SVG is inlined into the DOM or used as a CSS `background-image`.** When an SVG is loaded through `<img src=\"partner-logo.svg\">` — or through `background-image: url(...)` in some engines — browsers render it in a restricted mode that refuses to fetch external resources. The photo half would silently render as an empty 80×80 gap. Since the data URIs are self-contained today, that failure mode does not exist yet, and this refactor is what would introduce it.\n\nSo:\n\n- **If the logo is consumed inline** (a React/Astro component, an SVG sprite): keep the single-file shape with an external `href` as above. Result: `partner-logo.svg` + `partner-photo.webp`.\n- **If it's consumed via `<img>`/CSS** — and honestly, this is the better end state regardless: stop nesting the photo. Shrink the SVG to the 80×80 vector mark alone and compose the two at the markup level. Result: `partner-logo.svg` (pure vector, self-contained, scales infinitely) + `partner-photo.webp` (a real raster the browser can cache, lazy-load, and serve responsively via `srcset`) sitting next to each other in a flex container with a 20px gap. Each asset then gets the loading and caching treatment its own type deserves — which the nested version forecloses entirely.\n\nGrepping the fixture turns up no importers of `partner-logo.svg`, so pick based on how the real consumer loads it. The file stays shipped either way.\n\n## One caveat on the framing\n\nThe finding's boilerplate warns that an embedded raster is \"usually larger than a plain PNG.\" At 592 bytes total, that isn't the story here — these payloads are stubs, and you will not win measurable page weight. The reasons to do this refactor are the structural ones: the vector half genuinely scales at every DPI instead of being pinned to 80×80, and the photo half becomes an asset the browser can cache and serve responsively rather than an opaque base64 blob that gets re-downloaded with every copy of the SVG. Worth doing, but bank it as asset-pipeline correctness, not a performance fix.",
